# Layers

```text
middleware → route → controller → service → model → MongoDB
```

| File | May import | Must not import |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `routes/*` | matching controller, schemas, middleware | models, services |
| `controllers/*` | matching service, `lib/response`, `lib/errors`, `config/env` | models |
| `auth.service.ts` | `user.model`, `session.model`, `lib` | doctor, patient, or dashboard files, Express |
| `doctor.service.ts` | `doctor.model`, `patient.model` count functions, `lib` | `patient.service`, auth, Express |
| `patient.service.ts` | `patient.model`, `doctor.model.findById` and `findByIds`, `lib` | `doctor.service`, auth, Express |
| `dashboard.service.ts` | doctor and patient model query functions, `lib` | other services, Express |
| `models/*` | `mongoose` | services, controllers, routes, Express |
| `middleware/require-admin.ts` | `lib/tokens`, `lib/errors` | models |

A route declares the path and middleware, then hands off to a controller. A controller reads validated input, calls one service function, and writes one response helper. It does not catch `DomainError`. Refresh clears `dt_refresh` and then rethrows. `async-handler` on the route forwards rejections to `error-handler`.

`GET /doctors/:doctorId/patients` is declared on the doctor router. Its controller calls `patient.service`. The doctor service does not import the patient service. Health has no service; `health.controller.ts` calls `pingDatabase`.

```typescript
doctorRouter.post("/", validate({ body: createDoctorBodySchema }), asyncHandler(create));

export async function create(_req: Request, res: Response): Promise<void> {
  const doctor = await doctorService.createDoctor(res.locals.body as CreateDoctorBody);
  sendData(res, 201, "Doctor created successfully", doctor);
}
```

Relative imports use the `.js` suffix. Named exports. Controllers, services, and models are async functions, not classes. `app.ts` mounts routers and does not inject repositories.
